INT. MOBILE DOG-GROOMING VAN - STUCK IN AUTOMATIC CAR WASH - DAY

Soap pours down the windshield in sheets. Brushes thump on the roof. Inside, a cramped cabin of clippers, towels, and a tiny steel tub where a very large poodle sits looking mortified.

GWENDOLYN "WENDY" OBUYA, forties, groomer, in a rubber apron, grips the steering wheel with both hands. Beside her, TRISTAN FAIRCLOUGH, twenties, her apprentice, holds a phone to his ear.

WENDY
Tell me they're turning it off.

TRISTAN
They're, um, looking at it.

LENNY (V.O.)
(tinny, bored)
Sir, the system shows your vehicle as "paid but not exited." That's a process state.

TRISTAN
Can you turn the brushes off?

LENNY (V.O.)
That's a process state.

WENDY
(to the phone)
What does that mean?

LENNY (V.O.)
It means you're in the process.

Wendy closes her eyes. The brushes thump louder. The poodle whines.

TRISTAN
It's okay, Duchess. It's okay.

WENDY
Don't talk to her. She knows we're lying.

LENNY (V.O.)
Sir, do you want the premium rinse? You're getting it anyway.

Click. The line dies. The van lurches forward a foot and stops.

WENDY
I swear to God.

The side door slides open. A man in a drenched uniform pushes in, one arm over his head against the spray: LEONARD "LENNY" BASKERVILLE, sixties, wearing a name badge that says MANAGER and a visible pool of soap in his hair.

LENNY
Who's in charge of this vehicle?

WENDY
Me. Why are you in my van?

LENNY
Because it's in my wash.

TRISTAN
There's a dog in here, sir.

LENNY
I can see that. Is it licensed?

WENDY
For what?

LENNY
For the wash.

The brushes thump. The poodle sneezes a mouthful of foam. Lenny looks at it, and the dog looks back.

Wendy takes her foot off the brake.

WENDY
Hold on to something, Lenny.

LENNY
What?

She guns the engine. The van lurches, crunching through a plastic barrier, and bursts out the far end of the tunnel into daylight, trailing foam and a small yelp of triumph from Duchess.

LENNY (V.O.)
(shouting over the brushes)
I said thank you for choosing SparkleJet!
